<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Every operating system has conventions that its users know without being taught. On macOS the shortcut is ⌘K and
the accept button of a dialog is on the right; on Windows the same shortcut is Ctrl+K and the accept button is on
the left. An app downloads as a <code>.dmg</code>, an <code>.exe</code>, or a <code>.deb</code>. The share icon is
a box with an arrow on iOS and three connected dots on Android, and the app store badge is a different image on
each platform.

Media queries can describe what the device can do, but not whose conventions to follow. ''pointer/coarse'' doesn't
mean iOS: a Windows laptop with a touchscreen and an iPad both report it, and they want opposite button orders
and opposite shortcut labels. Feature queries can't help either, because the question isn't whether the engine
supports something, it's what the user's platform calls it and expects it to look like.

So pages work around it. They detect the platform once in script and write a class onto the root element, which is
what Visual Studio Code does, and what the user agent parsing libraries exist to feed: in one week of September
2026, <code>bowser</code> was downloaded about 42 million times and <code>ua-parser-js</code> about 23 million.
Firefox needed the same thing for its own interface, and added a <code>-moz-platform</code> media feature
[[MOZ-PLATFORM]] that only its internal style sheets can use.

This specification gives style sheets the same thing, in a form that reveals nothing that the user agent doesn't
already send to every server in its <code>User-Agent</code> header.

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* Shortcut labels */
    .key-mod::after { content: "Ctrl"; }
    @media (platform: macos), (platform: ios) {
      .key-mod::after { content: "⌘"; }
    }

    /* Dialog buttons: accept last on Apple platforms, first on Windows */
    .dialog-buttons { display: flex; }
    @media (platform: windows) {
      .dialog-buttons .accept { order: -1; }
    }

    /* The right download, and the right store badge */
    .download-dmg, .download-deb, .badge-app-store, .badge-play { display: none; }
    @media (platform: macos) { .download-exe { display: none; } .download-dmg { display: inline; } }
    @media (platform: linux) { .download-exe { display: none; } .download-deb { display: inline; } }
    @media (platform: ios) { .badge-play { display: none; } .badge-app-store { display: inline; } }
    </pre>
</div>

The feature reports a family, never a version, and never a device. It is deliberately not a way to ask what the
browser can do: that is what feature queries are for, and [[#security-not-detection]] explains why using it that
way breaks browsers.


<h2 id=feature>The '@media/platform' feature</h2>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: platform
Value: windows | macos | ios | android | linux | chromeos | other
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The '@media/platform' media feature is a [=system media feature=]. Its [=actual value=] is the keyword for the
document's [=reported platform=]:

: <dfn value for=@media/platform>windows</dfn>
:: Microsoft Windows.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>macos</dfn>
:: Apple macOS.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>ios</dfn>
:: Apple iOS and iPadOS.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>android</dfn>
:: Android, including systems built on it whose conventions are Android's.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>linux</dfn>
:: A Linux or Unix-like desktop system that isn't one of the above.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>chromeos</dfn>
:: ChromeOS.
: <dfn value for=@media/platform>other</dfn>
:: Any other platform, and any platform the user agent chooses not to identify.

In a [=boolean context=], the feature is always true.

The list of keywords is closed. A user agent must report ''platform/other'' for a platform that has no keyword,
and must not invent keywords. New keywords are added by revising this specification.

<div class=note>
    A closed list keeps the feature from identifying the users of uncommon systems. If every system had its own
    keyword, the few users of one would be easy to recognize, while the feature is worth little to them, because
    an author can't design for a platform they have never seen. See [[#privacy-closed-list]].
</div>

<h3 id=platform-source>The reported platform</h3>

The <dfn export>reported platform</dfn> of a [=document=] is the platform that the user agent presents to that
document's origin elsewhere. It is not necessarily the platform the user agent is running on.

A user agent must determine the reported platform as follows:

1. If the user agent implements <code>navigator.userAgentData</code> [[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]], the reported platform is
    the value of its <code>platform</code> property for the document, mapped with the table below.
1. Otherwise, the reported platform is the platform identified by the <code>User-Agent</code> header the user agent
    would send with a request for the document.

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th><code>navigator.userAgentData.platform</code><th>'@media/platform'
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>"Android"<td>''platform/android''
        <tr><td>"Chrome OS", "Chromium OS"<td>''platform/chromeos''
        <tr><td>"iOS"<td>''platform/ios''
        <tr><td>"Linux"<td>''platform/linux''
        <tr><td>"macOS"<td>''platform/macos''
        <tr><td>"Windows"<td>''platform/windows''
        <tr><td>Any other value, including "Unknown" and "Fuchsia"<td>''platform/other''
</table>

The feature must agree with what the user agent reports elsewhere, even when that isn't the platform it is running
on. In particular:

* If the user agent presents itself as another platform, as browsers do in a "request desktop site" mode, the
    feature reports that other platform. On iPadOS, Safari's desktop-class browsing presents itself as macOS, so
    the feature is ''platform/macos''. In Chrome on Android, desktop mode presents itself as Linux, so the feature
    is ''platform/linux''.
* If the user agent normalizes or hides the platform to protect the user from fingerprinting, the feature reports
    the normalized platform, and reports ''platform/other'' if the user agent identifies no platform.

<div class=note>
    This rule is what makes the feature safe: it can't disagree with the <code>User-Agent</code> header, with
    <code>navigator.userAgentData.platform</code>, or with the user's choice of desktop mode, so it never tells
    anyone anything they couldn't already learn. It also means the feature is wrong exactly when the user agent is
    already wrong, which authors can't do anything about, and users have asked for.
</div>

<h3 id=changes>Changes</h3>

The value is determined when the [=document=] is created, and doesn't change while the document exists.

<div class=note>
    Turning "request desktop site" on or off reloads the page, so there is no need for the value to change, and
    nothing that could link two documents by the moment it changed.
</div>

<h3 id=reduced-exposure>Reduced exposure</h3>

In a [=reduced-exposure context=], the feature follows the same rule: it reports the [=reported platform=]. A user
agent that already normalizes the platform it presents, in that context or generally, gets the same normalization
here for free.

<div class=note>
    There is nothing to take away. A style sheet that reports the value to a server tells it what the server read
    in the <code>User-Agent</code> header of the request that fetched the style sheet.
</div>


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

<h3 id=privacy-exposed>What the feature reveals</h3>

The feature reveals which of seven operating system families the user agent presents itself as. It doesn't reveal:

* the version of the operating system, or its build, edition, or distribution;
* the device model, the manufacturer, the CPU architecture, or the bitness;
* the browser, its engine, or its version;
* whether the reported platform is the real one.

<h3 id=privacy-nothing-new>Why it adds nothing</h3>

The platform is already available to everyone who can see a request or run script:

* Every request the document makes carries a <code>User-Agent</code> header that identifies the platform family.
    Even after Chromium's User-Agent Reduction, which froze the platform version, the architecture, and the device
    model, the header still identifies the family: <code>Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64</code>,
    <code>Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_15_7</code>, <code>X11; Linux x86_64</code>,
    <code>X11; CrOS x86_64 14541.0.0</code>, or <code>Linux; Android 10; K</code> [[UA-REDUCTION]].
* <code>Sec-CH-UA-Platform</code> is one of the three client hints that user agents implementing
    [[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]] send on every request by default, because the platform name on its own is low entropy.
* Script can read <code>navigator.userAgentData.platform</code> and <code>navigator.platform</code>.

Because [[#platform-source]] requires the feature to agree with those, it reaches no context that they don't.
This includes documents where script is disabled, such as messages in an email client: the request that fetched the
message, and the request a style sheet would make to report the value, both carry the same header.

<h3 id=privacy-fingerprinting>Fingerprinting</h3>

The platform family is one of the cheapest parts of the user agent string. Measured across large fingerprint
collections, the whole <code>User-Agent</code> string carries about 10 bits of identifying information in
Panopticlick's 2010 data, 9.8 bits in AmIUnique's 2016 data, and 7.2 bits in the 2018 "Hiding in the Crowd"
data set of over two million fingerprints; <code>navigator.platform</code>, on its own, carries 2.3 bits and 1.2
bits in the last two [[FINGERPRINT-SURVEY]] [[HIDING-IN-THE-CROWD]]. Almost all of the entropy of a user agent
string is in versions and device models, which this feature doesn't report.

The value is also stable, changes only when the user changes platforms, and is the same for large numbers of users.
It adds nothing to a fingerprint that the same page's requests didn't already carry.

<h3 id=privacy-closed-list>The closed list</h3>

An open list would punish the users of uncommon systems. If the feature had a keyword for every system, a user on
one that a thousandth of users run would reveal about 10 bits, while an author who has never seen that system
can't style for it anyway. Every such system reports ''platform/other'', which is also what a user agent reports
when it doesn't want to identify the platform at all.

<h3 id=privacy-normalization>User agents that normalize the platform</h3>

Tor Browser normalizes rather than randomizes: all Windows users appear as one Windows version, all macOS users as
one macOS version, and all Linux and BSD systems as one. It used to disguise the family itself, and stopped,
because the benefit was "a negligible amount of benefit to the user in terms of additional linkability
protections" against real site breakage [[TOR-UA-SPOOFING]]. Firefox's resist-fingerprinting mode likewise reports
the latest release version but doesn't spoof the operating system.

This feature inherits whatever the user agent decides, including a decision to report ''platform/other'' everywhere.

<h3 id=privacy-third-party>Embedded content</h3>

The feature has the same value in cross-origin frames as in the top-level document, because a frame's own requests
carry the same <code>User-Agent</code> header and its script can read the same values.

<h3 id=privacy-residual>Remaining risks</h3>

* **Entrenchment.** Making platform styling easy makes platform sniffing more common, and sniffing tends to
    outlive its reason. [[#security-not-detection]] and [[#alt-conventions]] discuss this.
* **Style-only contexts.** A style sheet can report the value without script. It reports a fact the same server
    already received in a header, but it does so without the page's script being involved, which a site's own
    content security policy might otherwise have limited.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

<h3 id=security-not-detection>It is not feature detection</h3>

The platform doesn't say which CSS or JavaScript features work. Sites that decide what to send from the platform
break browsers that the site's author didn't test, which is the reason user agent strings are full of the names of
other browsers. Authors should use ''@supports'', <code>@media</code> capability features such as
'@media/pointer' and '@media/hover', and script feature detection, and use this feature only for conventions:
what to call a key, which order to put buttons in, which asset to show. [[DESIGN-PRINCIPLES]] says features should
be detectable, and this one deliberately doesn't help with that.

<h3 id=security-targeting>Targeting</h3>

A page can show different content to users of one platform, such as an attack that tells Windows users to press a
key combination that runs a command. Attackers already do this from the server, using the
<code>User-Agent</code> header, so the feature adds no reach. What it adds is that the variation can happen
entirely in a style sheet, so a service that checks the page by fetching it once, from one platform, sees only one
version. Checking services should render pages as more than one platform.

<h3 id=security-not-a-decision>Not an input to decisions that matter</h3>

The user controls the value, through desktop mode, through browser settings, and by choosing a browser that
normalizes it. Pages must not use it for licensing, entitlement, fraud scoring, or any security decision.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-conventions>Features for the conventions themselves</h3>

Instead of naming the platform, CSS could expose each convention: a feature for how the modifier key is written, one
for the order of dialog buttons, one for the direction of the system back gesture. That would say what authors
actually need, would survive a platform changing its conventions, and matches how the CSS Working Group has
handled other requests, by turning "what device is this" into "what does it do". It was not chosen here because the
list of conventions is long and open-ended, and because assets such as store badges and installer formats aren't
conventions the user agent knows about. This is the strongest alternative, and reviewers may prefer it.

<h3 id=alt-version>The operating system version</h3>

A feature for the version would let a page match the platform's current design language. It is not proposed:
the version is where almost all of the fingerprinting value of a user agent string is, Safari froze the macOS
version in its user agent string in 2020 and now reports <code>Intel Mac OS X 10_15_7</code> permanently,
Chromium's User-Agent Reduction froze it too [[UA-REDUCTION]], and <code>Sec-CH-UA-Platform-Version</code> is a
high-entropy hint that a site has to ask for. Matching a design language is better served by the features that
describe it, such as '@media/prefers-reduced-transparency' and '@media/prefers-contrast'.

<h3 id=alt-model-arch>The device model, architecture, and bitness</h3>

A download page would like to offer an Apple Silicon or an Intel build, and <code>Sec-CH-UA-Arch</code> exists for
that. These are not proposed: they are high-entropy hints that a site must request, the user agent is told to
report them as empty unless a binary download is likely [[UA-CLIENT-HINTS]], and choosing a download link is
something a page can do with script or on the server, where the hint already is.

<h3 id=alt-browser>The browser or its engine</h3>

A feature for the browser brand or engine is not proposed. Feature queries answer the question that browser
sniffing is usually asking, sniffing entrenches the assumptions that break new browsers, and engines have been
closing the CSS-only browser detection channels that exist rather than opening new ones. A page that needs to work
around an engine bug can usually detect the bug.

<h3 id=alt-media-type>A media type</h3>

CSS once had media types for device classes, such as <code>@media tv</code> and <code>@media handheld</code>, and
they were deprecated: they made one keyword stand for a whole bundle of assumptions. This feature deliberately
stands for one thing, the platform's conventions, and nothing else. Device classes are handled separately by
[[FORM-FACTOR-MEDIA-QUERY]].


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>On iPadOS, Safari presents itself as macOS, so an iPad reports ''platform/macos'' and gets macOS
conventions, including a ⌘ label that matches an attached keyboard but not the on-screen one. Should the
specification say anything, given that the alternative is disagreeing with the user agent string?

<p class=issue>Should ''platform/chromeos'' exist, or should ChromeOS report ''platform/linux''? It has its own
conventions, and <code>Sec-CH-UA-Platform</code> distinguishes it, but it is also the only desktop platform here
with a single browser.

<p class=issue>Firefox restricted its own theme-related media features to internal style sheets in Firefox 87
[[MOZ-787521]], on the grounds that they expose system configuration without script. Does the argument in
[[#privacy-nothing-new]] answer that objection for this feature, given that those features exposed settings that
the <code>User-Agent</code> header doesn't carry?


<h2 id=questionnaire class=no-num>Appendix: Security and privacy questionnaire</h2>

*This section is non-normative.* These are the answers to the questions in
[[SECURITY-PRIVACY-QUESTIONNAIRE]].

: What information does this feature expose, and for what purposes?
:: Which of seven operating system families the user agent presents itself as, so pages can follow that platform's
    conventions.
: Do features in your specification expose the minimum amount of information necessary?
:: Yes. A closed list of families, with no version, model, architecture, or browser, and the value must match what
    the <code>User-Agent</code> header already carries.
: Do the features expose personal information, PII, or information derived from either?
:: No. The platform family suggests little about a person, though it correlates weakly with income and occupation.
: How do the features deal with sensitive information?
:: They expose none beyond what every request already carries.
: Does data exposed carry related but distinct information that may not be obvious to users?
:: Only weakly: that the user chose a desktop mode, since the reported platform then changes.
: Do the features introduce state that persists across browsing sessions?
:: No.
: Do the features expose information about the underlying platform to origins?
:: Yes, its family, to the same extent as the <code>User-Agent</code> header.
: Does this specification allow an origin to send data to the underlying platform?
:: No.
: Do features enable access to device sensors?
:: No.
: Do features enable new script execution or loading mechanisms?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin to access other devices?
:: No.
: Do features allow an origin some measure of control over a user agent's native UI?
:: No.
: What temporary identifiers do the features create or expose to the web?
:: None. The value doesn't change while a document exists.
: How does this specification distinguish between behavior in first-party and third-party contexts?
:: It doesn't, because a cross-origin frame's own requests carry the same header.
: How do the features work in the context of a browser's Private Browsing or Incognito mode?
:: The same as the <code>User-Agent</code> header does in that mode. A user agent that normalizes the platform
    there reports the normalized value here.
: Does this specification have both "Security Considerations" and "Privacy Considerations" sections?
:: Yes.
: Do features enable origins to downgrade default security protections?
:: No.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature is kept alive in BFCache?
:: It keeps the value it was created with, as its <code>User-Agent</code> header would.
: What happens when a document that uses your feature gets disconnected?
:: Nothing is kept.
: Does your spec define when and how new kinds of errors should be raised?
:: No.
: Does your feature allow sites to learn about the user's use of assistive technology?
:: No.
: What should this questionnaire have asked?
:: Whether a feature encourages a practice, such as user-agent sniffing, whose cost falls on browsers and users
    that the author never tested.
